---
title: "AI-assisted development"
sidebar:
  order: 6
---

NAHPU welcomes AI-assisted development when it is used as an engineering tool
under human direction. AI can help contributors explore unfamiliar code, draft
tests, generate small implementation options, and speed up repetitive work. It
does not replace domain understanding, code review, testing, or accountability.

## Why NAHPU needs careful engineering

NAHPU supports natural history fieldwork, and natural history work is
inherently complex. A single collecting record can connect biology, taxonomy,
geography, time, personnel, field methods, morphology, specimen preparation,
media metadata, permitting, and downstream statistics. The app has to preserve
those relationships while remaining usable in remote field conditions.

This complexity is not accidental:

- Biodiversity infrastructure such as [GBIF](https://www.gbif.org/what-is-gbif)
  brings together data from museum specimens, DNA barcodes, observations, and
  other sources using shared standards and tools.
- [Darwin Core](https://www.tdwg.org/standards/dwc/) exists because biological
  diversity data needs shared identifiers, labels, and definitions for taxa,
  occurrences, specimens, samples, and related information.
- Specimen data often depends on multiple disciplines at once: taxonomy for
  names and identifications, geography and GIS for localities and coordinates,
  ecology for habitat and sampling context, morphology and physiology for
  measurements, statistics for summaries and analyses, and informatics for data
  validation, import, export, and interoperability.

Because of this, many changes that look small in the UI can affect data
integrity. For example, changing an export field can affect Darwin Core mapping;
changing coordinate handling can affect GIS exports; changing date or time
handling can affect field chronology; changing database schema can affect all
existing projects.

## How to use AI well

Use AI tools to accelerate work that you can still understand, verify, and
maintain.

Good uses include:

- asking for a map of relevant files before editing;
- generating a first draft of a focused test;
- comparing two implementation approaches;
- explaining unfamiliar Flutter, Drift, Rust, or GIS code;
- finding edge cases for import, export, backup, or migration logic;
- drafting documentation that you then fact-check and edit.

Poor uses include:

- pasting generated code into NAHPU without understanding it;
- accepting generated migrations without testing old database versions;
- changing export behavior without validating output files;
- adding dependencies suggested by AI without checking maintenance, license, and
  platform impact;
- using AI-generated scientific or taxonomic claims without a source.

## Required contributor responsibility

If you use AI while contributing to NAHPU, you are still the author of the
change.

You must be able to:

- explain the problem being solved;
- explain the code path that changed;
- explain any generated code you kept;
- describe the domain assumptions behind the change;
- run and report the relevant checks;
- respond to review comments without delegating judgment to AI.

Pull requests submitted by AI agents, bot accounts, or contributors who cannot
explain and maintain the submitted code will be rejected. We also reject
contributions that appear to be copied from AI output without review,
integration with existing NAHPU patterns, or appropriate tests.

{% aside type="caution" title="AI-assisted development is not vibe coding" %}
We use AI as an assistant, not as an accountable developer. Merriam-Webster
describes vibe coding as careless AI-assisted coding where a person tells AI
what to build and may not understand how or why the code works. That is not
acceptable for NAHPU. Production contributions must be reviewed, understood,
tested, and owned by a human contributor.
{% /aside %}

## Pull request expectations

When AI helped with a contribution, include a short note in the pull request if
it materially shaped the implementation.

Useful disclosure examples:

- "AI helped identify related files; implementation and tests were written and
  verified manually."
- "AI drafted the first version of the widget test; I revised it and confirmed
  it fails without the fix."
- "AI helped compare two migration strategies; this PR uses the smaller
  additive migration described below."

The disclosure is not a penalty. It helps reviewers understand the development
process and focus review on the parts most likely to need scrutiny.

## Extra care areas

Be especially careful when using AI around:

- database migrations;
- import, export, and backup code;
- coordinate, locality, and GIS transformations;
- taxonomic names and biological terminology;
- date, time, timezone, and chronology handling;
- generated Flutter Rust Bridge bindings;
- platform-specific file access on Android, iOS, macOS, Windows, and Linux.

For these areas, prefer smaller changes, stronger tests, and source-backed
documentation.

